#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:10
msgid "3.2 The Intractable Partition Function Problem"
msgstr "3.2 The Intractable Partition Function Problem"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:12
msgid ""
"Section [3.1 Defining the Objective: Low Energy for Real Data](kpp-"
"theoretical-foundations-ebms-def.md) introduced the maximum likelihood "
"objective for energy-based models and revealed that the gradient of the "
"negative log-likelihood contains a term involving an expectation under the "
"model distribution. This expectation, in turn, requires summing over all "
"possible configurations, a sum that is encoded in the **partition function**"
" $Z_\\theta$. In this section, we examine why the partition function is "
"computationally intractable, what consequences this intractability has for "
"learning and inference, and why it constitutes the central algorithmic "
"challenge for Boltzmann machines and energy-based models more broadly."
msgstr ""
"Section [3.1 Defining the Objective: Low Energy for Real Data](kpp-"
"theoretical-foundations-ebms-def.md) introduced the maximum likelihood "
"objective for energy-based models and revealed that the gradient of the "
"negative log-likelihood contains a term involving an expectation under the "
"model distribution. This expectation, in turn, requires summing over all "
"possible configurations, a sum that is encoded in the **partition function**"
" $Z_\\theta$. In this section, we examine why the partition function is "
"computationally intractable, what consequences this intractability has for "
"learning and inference, and why it constitutes the central algorithmic "
"challenge for Boltzmann machines and energy-based models more broadly."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:14
msgid "The Partition Function: Definition and Its Implications"
msgstr "The Partition Function: Definition and Its Implications"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:15
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Recall the definition of the partition function for a Boltzmann machine with"
" binary units $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\{0,1\\}^N$:"
msgstr ""
"Recall the definition of the partition function for a Boltzmann machine with"
" binary units $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\{0,1\\}^N$:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:17
msgid ""
"Z_\\theta = \\sum_{\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}} \\in \\{0,1\\}^N} "
"\\exp\\left(-E_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}})\\right)"
msgstr ""
"Z_\\theta = \\sum_{\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}} \\in \\{0,1\\}^N} "
"\\exp\\left(-E_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}})\\right)"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:19
msgid ""
"The sum runs over **all** $2^N$ **possible binary vectors** of length $N$. "
"For a modest network of $N=100$ visible units (a tiny image by modern "
"standards, only $10 \\times 10$ pixels), the number of terms in this sum is:"
msgstr ""
"The sum runs over **all** $2^N$ **possible binary vectors** of length $N$. "
"For a modest network of $N=100$ visible units (a tiny image by modern "
"standards, only $10 \\times 10$ pixels), the number of terms in this sum is:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:21
#, python-brace-format
msgid "2^{100} \\approx 1.27 \\times 10^{30}"
msgstr "2^{100} \\approx 1.27 \\times 10^{30}"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:23
msgid "To put this number in perspective:"
msgstr "To put this number in perspective:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:25
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The estimated number of stars in the observable universe is about $10^{22}$ "
"to $10^{24}$."
msgstr ""
"The estimated number of stars in the observable universe is about $10^{22}$ "
"to $10^{24}$."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:26
#, python-brace-format
msgid "The age of the universe is approximately $4.35 \\times 10^{17}$ seconds."
msgstr ""
"The age of the universe is approximately $4.35 \\times 10^{17}$ seconds."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:27
msgid ""
"Even if we could evaluate one trillion ($10^{12}$) configurations per "
"second, enumerating $2^{100}$ configurations would take \\[ "
"\\frac{2^{100}}{10^{12}}\\approx 1.27\\times 10^{18}\\ \\text{seconds}, \\] "
"about \\(3\\) times the age of the universe. At a more modest rate of "
"\\(10^9\\) configurations per second, it would take \\[ "
"\\frac{2^{100}}{10^9}\\approx 1.27\\times 10^{21}\\ \\text{seconds}, \\] "
"about \\(2.9\\times 10^3\\) times the age of the universe."
msgstr ""
"Even if we could evaluate one trillion ($10^{12}$) configurations per "
"second, enumerating $2^{100}$ configurations would take \\[ "
"\\frac{2^{100}}{10^{12}}\\approx 1.27\\times 10^{18}\\ \\text{seconds}, \\] "
"about \\(3\\) times the age of the universe. At a more modest rate of "
"\\(10^9\\) configurations per second, it would take \\[ "
"\\frac{2^{100}}{10^9}\\approx 1.27\\times 10^{21}\\ \\text{seconds}, \\] "
"about \\(2.9\\times 10^3\\) times the age of the universe."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:38
msgid ""
"And $N = 100$ is **tiny**. Real-world Boltzmann machines and restricted "
"Boltzmann machines often have hundreds of visible units and hundreds or "
"thousands of hidden units, yielding state spaces of size $2^{N_{\\text{vis}}"
" + N_{\\text{hid}}}$. The partition function is **exponentially large** in "
"the number of units."
msgstr ""
"And $N = 100$ is **tiny**. Real-world Boltzmann machines and restricted "
"Boltzmann machines often have hundreds of visible units and hundreds or "
"thousands of hidden units, yielding state spaces of size $2^{N_{\\text{vis}}"
" + N_{\\text{hid}}}$. The partition function is **exponentially large** in "
"the number of units."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:40
msgid "Why the Partition Function Matters"
msgstr "Why the Partition Function Matters"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:41
msgid "The partition function appears in three critical contexts:"
msgstr "The partition function appears in three critical contexts:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:43
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**Probability Evaluation**  To compute the probability "
"$P_\\theta(\\mathbf{x})$ of a specific configuration, we must divide the "
"Boltzmann factor by $Z_\\theta$:"
msgstr ""
"**Probability Evaluation**  To compute the probability "
"$P_\\theta(\\mathbf{x})$ of a specific configuration, we must divide the "
"Boltzmann factor by $Z_\\theta$:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:46
msgid "P_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}) = \\frac{\\exp(-E_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}))}{Z_\\theta}"
msgstr "P_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}) = \\frac{\\exp(-E_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}))}{Z_\\theta}"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:48
msgid ""
"Without $Z_\\theta$, we have only an unnormalized score. This means we "
"cannot directly compute likelihoods on test data, making model comparison "
"and hyperparameter tuning difficult."
msgstr ""
"Without $Z_\\theta$, we have only an unnormalized score. This means we "
"cannot directly compute likelihoods on test data, making model comparison "
"and hyperparameter tuning difficult."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:50
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**Learning by Gradient Descent**  Recall from Section [3.1 Defining the "
"Objective: Low Energy for Real Data](kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-"
"def.md) that the gradient of the negative log-likelihood (Eq. {eq}`eq-nll-"
"gradient-tf`, Section 3.1) decomposes into a data term and a model term:"
msgstr ""
"**Learning by Gradient Descent**  Recall from Section [3.1 Defining the "
"Objective: Low Energy for Real Data](kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-"
"def.md) that the gradient of the negative log-likelihood (Eq. {eq}`eq-nll-"
"gradient-tf`, Section 3.1) decomposes into a data term and a model term:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:53
msgid ""
"\\nabla f(\\theta) = -\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{data}} \\left[ \\frac{\\partial "
"E_\\theta}{\\partial \\theta} \\right] + \\mathbb{E}_{\\text{model}} \\left["
" \\frac{\\partial E_\\theta}{\\partial \\theta} \\right]"
msgstr ""
"\\nabla f(\\theta) = -\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{data}} \\left[ \\frac{\\partial "
"E_\\theta}{\\partial \\theta} \\right] + \\mathbb{E}_{\\text{model}} \\left["
" \\frac{\\partial E_\\theta}{\\partial \\theta} \\right]"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:55
msgid ""
"Note: $\\nabla f(\\theta) = -\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial "
"\\theta}$, where $\\mathcal{L}$ is the negative log-likelihood minimized in "
"many textbooks."
msgstr ""
"Note: $\\nabla f(\\theta) = -\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial "
"\\theta}$, where $\\mathcal{L}$ is the negative log-likelihood minimized in "
"many textbooks."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:57
msgid ""
"The first term is straightforward to estimate from the training set. The "
"second term, i.e. the expectation under the model distribution $P_\\theta$, "
"is the source of difficulty. As shown in the expression above, computing "
"this expectation exactly requires summing over all $2^N$ possible "
"configurations, weighted by the Boltzmann factor. Both the numerator and the"
" denominator involve the partition function $Z_\\theta$, making exact "
"gradient evaluation computationally intractable for any non-trivial model."
msgstr ""
"The first term is straightforward to estimate from the training set. The "
"second term, i.e. the expectation under the model distribution $P_\\theta$, "
"is the source of difficulty. As shown in the expression above, computing "
"this expectation exactly requires summing over all $2^N$ possible "
"configurations, weighted by the Boltzmann factor. Both the numerator and the"
" denominator involve the partition function $Z_\\theta$, making exact "
"gradient evaluation computationally intractable for any non-trivial model."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:59
msgid ""
"The **model expectation** $\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{model}}[\\cdot]$ *is an "
"average over the model distribution*$P_\\theta$:"
msgstr ""
"The **model expectation** $\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{model}}[\\cdot]$ *is an "
"average over the model distribution*$P_\\theta$:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:61
msgid ""
"\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{model}} \\left[ \\frac{\\partial E_\\theta}{\\partial "
"\\theta} \\right] = \\sum_{\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}} "
"P_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}) \\frac{\\partial "
"E_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}})}{\\partial \\theta} = \\frac{1}{Z_\\theta} "
"\\sum_{\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}} \\exp(-E_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}})) "
"\\frac{\\partial E_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}})}{\\partial \\theta}"
msgstr ""
"\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{model}} \\left[ \\frac{\\partial E_\\theta}{\\partial "
"\\theta} \\right] = \\sum_{\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}} "
"P_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}) \\frac{\\partial "
"E_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}})}{\\partial \\theta} = \\frac{1}{Z_\\theta} "
"\\sum_{\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}} \\exp(-E_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}})) "
"\\frac{\\partial E_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}})}{\\partial \\theta}"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:63
msgid ""
"Both the numerator and the denominator involve sums over all $2^N$ "
"configurations. Exact gradient computation is infeasible."
msgstr ""
"Both the numerator and the denominator involve sums over all $2^N$ "
"configurations. Exact gradient computation is infeasible."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:65
msgid ""
"**Sampling and Inference**  Generating samples from the model distribution "
"typically requires Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) methods, such as Gibbs "
"sampling. While MCMC does not require computing $Z_\\theta$ explicitly, it "
"does require running the Markov chain long enough to reach equilibrium. The "
"time required to obtain independent samples scales with the **mixing time** "
"of the chain, which can be exponentially long in the system size, especially"
" when the energy landscape has high barriers."
msgstr ""
"**Sampling and Inference**  Generating samples from the model distribution "
"typically requires Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) methods, such as Gibbs "
"sampling. While MCMC does not require computing $Z_\\theta$ explicitly, it "
"does require running the Markov chain long enough to reach equilibrium. The "
"time required to obtain independent samples scales with the **mixing time** "
"of the chain, which can be exponentially long in the system size, especially"
" when the energy landscape has high barriers."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:68
msgid "The Partition Function as a Free Energy Barrier"
msgstr "The Partition Function as a Free Energy Barrier"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:69
msgid ""
"In statistical physics, the partition function is intimately related to the "
"**Helmholtz free energy** $F = -\\log Z$."
msgstr ""
"In statistical physics, the partition function is intimately related to the "
"**Helmholtz free energy** $F = -\\log Z$."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:71
msgid ""
"(Setting $ k_B T = 1$ for notational simplicity.) The free energy summarizes"
" the balance between energy and entropy. For a given visible configuration "
"$\\mathbf{v}$ in a Boltzmann machine with hidden units $\\mathbf{h}$, the "
"**free energy** is defined analogously as $F_\\theta(\\mathbf{v}) = -\\log "
"\\sum_{\\tilde{\\mathbf{h}}} \\exp\\left(-E_\\theta(\\mathbf{v}, "
"\\tilde{\\mathbf{h}})\\right)$."
msgstr ""
"(Setting $ k_B T = 1$ for notational simplicity.) The free energy summarizes"
" the balance between energy and entropy. For a given visible configuration "
"$\\mathbf{v}$ in a Boltzmann machine with hidden units $\\mathbf{h}$, the "
"**free energy** is defined analogously as $F_\\theta(\\mathbf{v}) = -\\log "
"\\sum_{\\tilde{\\mathbf{h}}} \\exp\\left(-E_\\theta(\\mathbf{v}, "
"\\tilde{\\mathbf{h}})\\right)$."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:73
msgid ""
"The probability of a visible vector is then: $$P_\\theta(\\mathbf{v}) = "
"\\frac{\\exp(-F_\\theta(\\mathbf{v}))}{\\sum_{\\tilde{\\mathbf{v}}} "
"\\exp(-F_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{v}}))}$$"
msgstr ""
"The probability of a visible vector is then: $$P_\\theta(\\mathbf{v}) = "
"\\frac{\\exp(-F_\\theta(\\mathbf{v}))}{\\sum_{\\tilde{\\mathbf{v}}} "
"\\exp(-F_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{v}}))}$$"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:76
msgid ""
"Even with hidden units marginalized out, the denominator still sums over all"
" $2^{N_{\\text{vis}}}$ visible configurations. The intractability remains."
msgstr ""
"Even with hidden units marginalized out, the denominator still sums over all"
" $2^{N_{\\text{vis}}}$ visible configurations. The intractability remains."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:78
msgid "The Consequence: Exact Maximum Likelihood Is Impossible"
msgstr "The Consequence: Exact Maximum Likelihood Is Impossible"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:79
msgid ""
"The intractability of $Z_\\theta$ has a stark implication: **exact maximum "
"likelihood learning of a Boltzmann machine is computationally impossible for"
" all but the smallest toy problems.** Any practical training algorithm must "
"rely on approximations."
msgstr ""
"The intractability of $Z_\\theta$ has a stark implication: **exact maximum "
"likelihood learning of a Boltzmann machine is computationally impossible for"
" all but the smallest toy problems.** Any practical training algorithm must "
"rely on approximations."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:81
msgid ""
"This is not merely an engineering inconvenience; it is a fundamental "
"computational barrier. The partition function sum is a prototypical "
"#P-complete problem in computational complexity theory, meaning it is at "
"least as hard as counting the number of satisfying assignments to a Boolean "
"formula."
msgstr ""
"This is not merely an engineering inconvenience; it is a fundamental "
"computational barrier. The partition function sum is a prototypical "
"#P-complete problem in computational complexity theory, meaning it is at "
"least as hard as counting the number of satisfying assignments to a Boolean "
"formula."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:83
msgid "Hessian and Non-Convexity"
msgstr "Hessian and Non-Convexity"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:85
msgid ""
"The difficulty extends beyond the gradient. The Hessian of the log-"
"likelihood function also reveals the complexity of the optimization "
"landscape. For a Boltzmann machine with only visible units, the Hessian has "
"a simple and revealing form:"
msgstr ""
"The difficulty extends beyond the gradient. The Hessian of the log-"
"likelihood function also reveals the complexity of the optimization "
"landscape. For a Boltzmann machine with only visible units, the Hessian has "
"a simple and revealing form:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:87
#, python-brace-format
msgid "\\nabla^2 f(\\theta) = -\\text{Cov}_\\theta(\\mathbf{S})"
msgstr "\\nabla^2 f(\\theta) = -\\text{Cov}_\\theta(\\mathbf{S})"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:89
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"where $\\mathbf{S}$ is the vector of sufficient statistics (e.g., $X_i$ and "
"$X_i X_j$). This means the Hessian is negative definite, and the "
"optimization is **concave**—there are no non-global local minima when all "
"units are visible."
msgstr ""
"where $\\mathbf{S}$ is the vector of sufficient statistics (e.g., $X_i$ and "
"$X_i X_j$). This means the Hessian is negative definite, and the "
"optimization is **concave**—there are no non-global local minima when all "
"units are visible."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:91
msgid "However, when hidden units are introduced, the Hessian becomes:"
msgstr "However, when hidden units are introduced, the Hessian becomes:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:93
msgid ""
"\\nabla^2 f(\\theta) = "
"\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{target}}[\\text{Cov}_\\theta(\\mathbf{S}|\\mathbf{X})] -"
" \\text{Cov}_\\theta(\\mathbf{S})"
msgstr ""
"\\nabla^2 f(\\theta) = "
"\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{target}}[\\text{Cov}_\\theta(\\mathbf{S}|\\mathbf{X})] -"
" \\text{Cov}_\\theta(\\mathbf{S})"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:95
msgid ""
"The first term is the conditional covariance given the visible units, and "
"the second term is the unconditional covariance under the model. This "
"expression is **not** necessarily negative semidefinite, meaning that **the "
"log-likelihood is not concave** in the presence of hidden units. "
"Consequently, gradient-based methods can converge to local optima, and the "
"quality of the learned model depends crucially on the initialization and "
"optimization algorithm."
msgstr ""
"The first term is the conditional covariance given the visible units, and "
"the second term is the unconditional covariance under the model. This "
"expression is **not** necessarily negative semidefinite, meaning that **the "
"log-likelihood is not concave** in the presence of hidden units. "
"Consequently, gradient-based methods can converge to local optima, and the "
"quality of the learned model depends crucially on the initialization and "
"optimization algorithm."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:97
msgid "Approaches to Taming the Intractability"
msgstr "Approaches to Taming the Intractability"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:98
msgid ""
"Faced with this barrier, researchers have developed several families of "
"approximation strategies. Understanding these strategies is essential for "
"navigating the landscape of energy-based learning."
msgstr ""
"Faced with this barrier, researchers have developed several families of "
"approximation strategies. Understanding these strategies is essential for "
"navigating the landscape of energy-based learning."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:100
msgid ""
"**Sampling-Based Approximations**  Instead of summing over all "
"configurations, we can **sample** configurations from the model distribution"
" using MCMC. The model expectation is then approximated by an empirical "
"average over the samples: $$\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{model}} \\left[ "
"\\frac{\\partial E_\\theta}{\\partial \\theta} \\right] \\approx "
"\\frac{1}{K} \\sum_{k=1}^K \\frac{\\partial "
"E_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}^{(k)})}{\\partial \\theta}, \\quad "
"\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}^{(k)} \\sim P_\\theta$$"
msgstr ""
"**Sampling-Based Approximations**  Instead of summing over all "
"configurations, we can **sample** configurations from the model distribution"
" using MCMC. The model expectation is then approximated by an empirical "
"average over the samples: $$\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{model}} \\left[ "
"\\frac{\\partial E_\\theta}{\\partial \\theta} \\right] \\approx "
"\\frac{1}{K} \\sum_{k=1}^K \\frac{\\partial "
"E_\\theta(\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}^{(k)})}{\\partial \\theta}, \\quad "
"\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}^{(k)} \\sim P_\\theta$$"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:104
msgid ""
"The challenge is that obtaining unbiased samples requires running the Markov"
" chain to equilibrium for every gradient step, which is prohibitively slow. "
"**Contrastive divergence** (Section [3.3 Contrastive Divergence](kpp-"
"theoretical-foundations-ebms-cd.md)) and **persistent contrastive "
"divergence** address this by using short MCMC runs initialized from the data"
" or from previous model states."
msgstr ""
"The challenge is that obtaining unbiased samples requires running the Markov"
" chain to equilibrium for every gradient step, which is prohibitively slow. "
"**Contrastive divergence** (Section [3.3 Contrastive Divergence](kpp-"
"theoretical-foundations-ebms-cd.md)) and **persistent contrastive "
"divergence** address this by using short MCMC runs initialized from the data"
" or from previous model states."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:106
msgid ""
"**Variational Approximations**  Variational methods replace the intractable "
"model distribution $P_\\theta$ with a simpler, tractable **variational "
"distribution** $Q_\\phi$ from a restricted family. The parameters $ \\phi$ "
"are optimized to make $Q_\\phi$ as close as possible to $P_\\theta$ in the "
"sense of Kullback-Leibler divergence. This transforms the learning problem "
"into an optimization over both $ \\theta$ and $\\phi$."
msgstr ""
"**Variational Approximations**  Variational methods replace the intractable "
"model distribution $P_\\theta$ with a simpler, tractable **variational "
"distribution** $Q_\\phi$ from a restricted family. The parameters $ \\phi$ "
"are optimized to make $Q_\\phi$ as close as possible to $P_\\theta$ in the "
"sense of Kullback-Leibler divergence. This transforms the learning problem "
"into an optimization over both $ \\theta$ and $\\phi$."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:109
msgid ""
"While variational methods provide a rigorous lower bound on the log-"
"likelihood, they often require making strong independence assumptions (e.g.,"
" mean-field approximations) that can limit the model's expressive power."
msgstr ""
"While variational methods provide a rigorous lower bound on the log-"
"likelihood, they often require making strong independence assumptions (e.g.,"
" mean-field approximations) that can limit the model's expressive power."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:111
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**Architectural Constraints**  By restricting the connectivity pattern of "
"the Boltzmann machine, we can make certain computations tractable. The "
"**Restricted Boltzmann Machine (RBM)** is the most celebrated example. By "
"eliminating visible-visible and hidden-hidden connections, the conditional "
"distributions $P(\\mathbf{h} \\mid \\mathbf{v})$ and $P(\\mathbf{v} \\mid "
"\\mathbf{h})$ factorize into products of independent Bernoulli "
"distributions. This allows efficient Gibbs sampling and enables the "
"contrastive divergence algorithm."
msgstr ""
"**Architectural Constraints**  By restricting the connectivity pattern of "
"the Boltzmann machine, we can make certain computations tractable. The "
"**Restricted Boltzmann Machine (RBM)** is the most celebrated example. By "
"eliminating visible-visible and hidden-hidden connections, the conditional "
"distributions $P(\\mathbf{h} \\mid \\mathbf{v})$ and $P(\\mathbf{v} \\mid "
"\\mathbf{h})$ factorize into products of independent Bernoulli "
"distributions. This allows efficient Gibbs sampling and enables the "
"contrastive divergence algorithm."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:114
msgid ""
"**Score Matching and Noise Contrastive Estimation**  These are alternative "
"training criteria that circumvent the partition function entirely. **Score "
"matching** minimizes the Fisher divergence between the model and data "
"distributions by matching the gradients of their log-densities, which does "
"not require $Z_\\theta$. **Noise contrastive estimation (NCE)** transforms "
"the unsupervised learning problem into a supervised logistic regression task"
" of distinguishing data samples from noise samples, where the partition "
"function becomes a learnable parameter."
msgstr ""
"**Score Matching and Noise Contrastive Estimation**  These are alternative "
"training criteria that circumvent the partition function entirely. **Score "
"matching** minimizes the Fisher divergence between the model and data "
"distributions by matching the gradients of their log-densities, which does "
"not require $Z_\\theta$. **Noise contrastive estimation (NCE)** transforms "
"the unsupervised learning problem into a supervised logistic regression task"
" of distinguishing data samples from noise samples, where the partition "
"function becomes a learnable parameter."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:117
msgid ""
"**Annealed Importance Sampling**  For model evaluation (rather than "
"training), **annealed importance sampling (AIS)** provides an unbiased "
"estimate of $Z_\\theta$. It works by gradually transforming a simple base "
"distribution (e.g., uniform) into the model distribution through a sequence "
"of intermediate distributions, using importance weights to correct for the "
"sampling bias. While computationally expensive, AIS is a gold standard for "
"evaluating generative models when exact likelihoods are required."
msgstr ""
"**Annealed Importance Sampling**  For model evaluation (rather than "
"training), **annealed importance sampling (AIS)** provides an unbiased "
"estimate of $Z_\\theta$. It works by gradually transforming a simple base "
"distribution (e.g., uniform) into the model distribution through a sequence "
"of intermediate distributions, using importance weights to correct for the "
"sampling bias. While computationally expensive, AIS is a gold standard for "
"evaluating generative models when exact likelihoods are required."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:120
msgid "The Partition Function in the Era of Deep Learning"
msgstr "The Partition Function in the Era of Deep Learning"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:121
msgid ""
"It is worth noting that the intractability of the partition function is not "
"unique to Boltzmann machines. Many modern deep generative models face "
"analogous challenges:"
msgstr ""
"It is worth noting that the intractability of the partition function is not "
"unique to Boltzmann machines. Many modern deep generative models face "
"analogous challenges:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:123
msgid ""
"**Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)** avoid the partition function "
"entirely by using an adversarial training criterion that does not require "
"likelihood evaluation."
msgstr ""
"**Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)** avoid the partition function "
"entirely by using an adversarial training criterion that does not require "
"likelihood evaluation."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:124
msgid ""
"**Variational Autoencoders (VAEs)** use a variational lower bound that "
"circumvents the partition function."
msgstr ""
"**Variational Autoencoders (VAEs)** use a variational lower bound that "
"circumvents the partition function."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:125
msgid ""
"**Normalizing Flows** design architectures where the Jacobian determinant, "
"the analog of $Z_\\theta$, is tractable by construction."
msgstr ""
"**Normalizing Flows** design architectures where the Jacobian determinant, "
"the analog of $Z_\\theta$, is tractable by construction."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:126
msgid ""
"**Diffusion Models** learn to reverse a gradual noising process, with a "
"training objective that involves only tractable conditional distributions."
msgstr ""
"**Diffusion Models** learn to reverse a gradual noising process, with a "
"training objective that involves only tractable conditional distributions."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:128
msgid ""
"The Boltzmann machine's struggle with the partition function thus "
"foreshadows a central theme in deep generative modeling: the tension between"
" expressive power and tractable normalization."
msgstr ""
"The Boltzmann machine's struggle with the partition function thus "
"foreshadows a central theme in deep generative modeling: the tension between"
" expressive power and tractable normalization."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:130
msgid "Summary"
msgstr "Summary"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:131
msgid ""
"The **partition function** $Z_\\theta = \\sum_{\\mathbf{x}} "
"\\exp(-E_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}))$ is a sum over an exponential number of "
"configurations, making exact computation infeasible for non-trivial models."
msgstr ""
"The **partition function** $Z_\\theta = \\sum_{\\mathbf{x}} "
"\\exp(-E_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}))$ is a sum over an exponential number of "
"configurations, making exact computation infeasible for non-trivial models."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:132
msgid ""
"The intractability of $Z_\\theta$ prevents exact **probability evaluation**,"
" exact **gradient computation**, and efficient exact **sampling**."
msgstr ""
"The intractability of $Z_\\theta$ prevents exact **probability evaluation**,"
" exact **gradient computation**, and efficient exact **sampling**."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:133
msgid ""
"This computational barrier implies that **exact maximum likelihood learning "
"is impossible** for general Boltzmann machines."
msgstr ""
"This computational barrier implies that **exact maximum likelihood learning "
"is impossible** for general Boltzmann machines."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:134
msgid ""
"Approximation strategies fall into several categories: **sampling-based** "
"(MCMC, contrastive divergence), **variational** (mean-field), "
"**architectural constraints** (RBM), and **alternative objectives** (score "
"matching, NCE)."
msgstr ""
"Approximation strategies fall into several categories: **sampling-based** "
"(MCMC, contrastive divergence), **variational** (mean-field), "
"**architectural constraints** (RBM), and **alternative objectives** (score "
"matching, NCE)."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:135
msgid ""
"The partition function problem is a fundamental challenge that connects "
"Boltzmann machines to contemporary deep generative models, many of which are"
" designed explicitly to circumvent or tame this intractability."
msgstr ""
"The partition function problem is a fundamental challenge that connects "
"Boltzmann machines to contemporary deep generative models, many of which are"
" designed explicitly to circumvent or tame this intractability."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-ebms-partition.md:136
msgid ""
"The **Restricted Boltzmann Machine** and **contrastive divergence,** topics "
"of the upcoming sections, provide a pragmatic and influential solution to "
"this problem for a restricted but powerful class of architectures."
msgstr ""
"The **Restricted Boltzmann Machine** and **contrastive divergence,** topics "
"of the upcoming sections, provide a pragmatic and influential solution to "
"this problem for a restricted but powerful class of architectures."

